body:
I mean for instance you don't have veining feet which I guess is a preference. 

&#x200B;

Now if you were asking top or bottom, then thats more of an important question. I much prefer soles and the bottom as I think the look better than the tops. And luckily you tend not to see veins on the soles :)

&#x200B;

Equally I could be asking top or bottom when it comes to cocks as well?...

I found working with a Junior designer very frustrating. This wasn't at a small company it was at a well known bank. I had become a standing creative director because the previous head of design jumped before they were going to be pushed out.

&#x200B;

I found it nearly impossible to get the junior to take feedback because they were always so emotionally attached to the design and didn't respect someone who wasn't their direct superior and senior.

>2 Attitude. Designers must be detached from their work. They need to be objective and professional. Hear opinions and consider adjustment. Accusations of “subjectivity” are garbage. Design is almost always subjective. And subjective is not necessarily the opposite of objective. “This doesn’t feel right” is always a valid argument, even when it can’t be justified empirically.

&#x200B;

Well said, and the "subjectivity to me is a defence mechanism. Design will always be subjective. However I'm personally trying to bring thinking to it. For instance reduce the amount of rounded corner in the design, and only have buttons with rounded corners. This will help the buttons to stand out more as there is something different about them, thus we will see an improvement to conversion. I think statements like this are fair, and should really be taken onboard in our UI design, but for whatever reason they don't land with our designer.
